\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.2}Method Overview and Representations}{79}{section.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:generator:method-overview}{{4.2}{79}{Method Overview and Representations}{section.4.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces  Overview of the proposed graph-conditioned denoising generator. The input graph is encoded into a padded node-level representation \(X_0\) and a graph-level condition vector \(C\). During training, a time \(t\) is sampled and noise is added to \(X_0\) according to a feature-wise schedule, producing \(X_t\). The conditional transformer receives \(X_t\), \(C\), and the time embedding \(e_t\), and produces node-level latents used by node-wise and pairwise prediction modules. At sampling time, a reverse noise schedule is followed from \(X_T\) to \(\hat  X_0\), reusing the same denoising model at each step. The generated node representation and edge probability matrix are decoded into a graph by the constrained decoder. }}{79}{figure.caption.93}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:generator:overview}{{4.1}{79}{Overview of the proposed graph-conditioned denoising generator. The input graph is encoded into a padded node-level representation \(X_0\) and a graph-level condition vector \(C\). During training, a time \(t\) is sampled and noise is added to \(X_0\) according to a feature-wise schedule, producing \(X_t\). The conditional transformer receives \(X_t\), \(C\), and the time embedding \(e_t\), and produces node-level latents used by node-wise and pairwise prediction modules. At sampling time, a reverse noise schedule is followed from \(X_T\) to \(\hat X_0\), reusing the same denoising model at each step. The generated node representation and edge probability matrix are decoded into a graph by the constrained decoder}{figure.caption.93}{}}
